## Generate Dynamic Transparent Blob Streams for GitHub Pages

Front-end developers often need to create and stream transparent assets for GitHub Pages. This guide explains how to use Blob objects and TypeScript to generate and stream transparent images, ensuring alpha channel integrity and browser compatibility. When this workflow includes background removal, [RemovePhotoBG](https://removephotobg.com/) can be used before export. Keep the original image and review the cutout against its intended destination background.

### Step 1: Set Up Your TypeScript Project

Create a new TypeScript project with the necessary dependencies. Ensure your project is configured to work with GitHub Pages. Use a build tool like Webpack or Vite to manage your assets and build process.

### Step 2: Create a Transparent Canvas

Use the HTML5 Canvas API to generate a transparent image. The canvas should be initialized with a transparent background to preserve the alpha channel. Set the canvas width and height to the desired dimensions.

```ts
const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
const width = 100;
const height = 100;

canvas.width = width;
canvas.height = height;

ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(255, 255, 25, 0.5)';
ctx.fillRect(0, 0, width, height);
```

### Step 3: Generate a Blob from the Canvas

Convert the canvas content into a Blob object. This Blob will represent the transparent image. Ensure the MIME type is set to 'image/png' to preserve the alpha channel.

```ts
const blob = await new Promise((resolve) => {
  canvas.toBlob(resolve, 'image/png');
});
```

### Step 4: Stream the Blob to the Client

Use the Blob object to create a downloadable link or stream the image directly to the client. Ensure the file type is correctly specified and the alpha channel is preserved during transmission.

```ts
const url = URL.createObjectURL(blob);
const link = document.createElement('a');
link.href = url;
link.download = 'transparent-image.png';
link.click();
URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
```

### Step 5: Test for Browser Compatibility

Verify that the generated Blob streams work across different browsers. Test on Chrome, Firefox, and Safari to ensure the alpha channel remains intact and the image is displayed correctly.

### Step 6: Deploy to GitHub Pages

Once the Blob streams are working locally, deploy your project to GitHub Pages. Ensure the build process includes the necessary assets and that the Blob generation logic is correctly integrated into your project structure.

By following these steps, you can create and stream dynamic transparent images for GitHub Pages using TypeScript and Blob objects. This approach ensures alpha channel integrity and compatibility across modern browsers.
